<h1>Indexing Conveyors for Precision Positioning</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">An indexing conveyor moves product in precise, repeatable start-stop cycles, advancing a fixed distance (the index length) and holding product stationary at a dwell position for filling, labeling, pick-and-place, assembly, or inspection. Buyers also call them positioning conveyors or conveyor positioning systems. Positioning accuracy ranges from ±2mm on simple pneumatic-stop systems to ±0.1mm on servo-driven belt or pallet-transfer indexers. Dorner, Shuttleworth, and mk North America manufacture indexing conveyors for packaging, pharmaceutical, and assembly applications.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Indexing Works</h2>
<p>Continuous conveyors move product at a constant speed. Indexing conveyors move product in a cycle: <strong>advance</strong>, <strong>decelerate</strong>, <strong>dwell</strong>, <strong>accelerate</strong>, and <strong>repeat</strong>. The dwell period gives downstream equipment, a filler nozzle, a labeler head, a pick-and-place arm, or an inspection camera, time to operate on a stationary target.</p>
<p>The indexing cycle has four critical parameters:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Index length (pitch):</strong> The distance the conveyor advances per cycle. This equals the spacing between product stations. Common index lengths range from 50mm (small bottles) to 1,200mm (pallet indexers).</li>
<li><strong>Index time:</strong> How long the conveyor takes to advance one index length. Shorter index times mean higher throughput. Typical range: 0.2 to 2.0 seconds.</li>
<li><strong>Dwell time:</strong> How long product stays stationary at the station. Determined by the downstream operation, a label application takes 0.1–0.3 seconds; a multi-head filler takes 1.0–3.0 seconds.</li>
<li><strong>Cycle time:</strong> Index time + dwell time = one complete cycle. Throughput (products per minute) = 60 / cycle time in seconds.</li>
</ul>
<p>The motion profile, how the conveyor accelerates, cruises, and decelerates, directly affects product stability. Aggressive acceleration topples tall bottles. Abrupt deceleration shifts loose product in trays. Servo-driven indexers let engineers tune the motion profile (trapezoidal, S-curve, or custom) to match the product's stability characteristics. Mechanical indexers use fixed cam profiles that balance speed and smoothness for a given application.</p>
<h2 id="tech-heading">Indexing Technologies</h2>
<h3>Servo Indexing Conveyors</h3>
<p>A servo indexing conveyor uses a servo motor to advance the belt a programmed distance, stop it at a programmed position, and repeat, with index length, speed, dwell, and motion profile all set in software. Servo-driven belt indexing is the most versatile and widely used indexing technology for packaging lines. A servo motor drives the conveyor belt through a precision gearbox. The servo controller executes programmable motion profiles, trapezoidal, S-curve, sinusoidal, or custom, with index length, speed, and dwell time all adjustable through the HMI. Dorner's 2200 Series Precision Move and mk North America's VersaMove indexing conveyors are examples of servo belt indexers used in packaging.</p>
<p>Servo belt indexers achieve ±0.1mm to ±0.5mm repeatability depending on belt type, drive ratio, and load. Timing belts (polyurethane with steel or Kevlar cords) provide the highest precision because they eliminate belt slip. Flat PVC or polyurethane belts offer lower friction for product sliding onto and off the belt but sacrifice positioning accuracy to ±0.5–1.0mm due to belt elasticity.</p>
<p>Changeover on servo belt indexers requires no mechanical adjustment. Operators load a new recipe, index length, speed, dwell, and motion profile, from the HMI. This makes servo belt indexing the default choice for lines running multiple SKUs with different package sizes. Dorner's SmartPace software automates recipe management and links indexing parameters to upstream and downstream machine speeds.</p>
<h3>Timing Screw (Starwheel) Indexing</h3>
<p>Timing screws (also called feed screws or worm drives) use a helical screw rotating alongside the conveyor to capture product and space it at a precise pitch. The screw's helix geometry determines the output spacing. Products ride on a continuous flat belt or <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller conveyor</a> while the screw controls spacing. This technology is dominant in bottle and container lines where round products need consistent pitch before labeling, filling, or capping.</p>
<p>Servo-driven timing screws adjust pitch by changing the screw speed relative to the belt speed. Fixed-pitch timing screws require a physical screw change for different container diameters. Changeover time for fixed screws ranges from 15 to 45 minutes. Servo screws change over in 1–2 minutes through recipe selection. Manufacturers including Hartness (part of ITW), Morrison Container Handling Solutions, and Aagard supply timing screws for packaging lines.</p>
<h3>Mechanical Cam &amp; Geneva Drive Indexing</h3>
<p>Mechanical indexing uses physical cams or Geneva drives to convert continuous motor rotation into intermittent motion. A Geneva drive produces a fixed dwell-to-index ratio, typically 2:1 or 3:1, with smooth acceleration and deceleration built into the cam geometry. The motion profile is fixed and repeatable without electronic control, making these systems extremely reliable for dedicated, single-format lines.</p>
<p>Cam-driven indexers use barrel cams, globoidal cams, or cylindrical cams to produce custom motion profiles. The cam grind determines the acceleration curve, dwell period, and maximum speed. Changing the motion profile requires machining a new cam, a process that takes weeks and costs significantly more than a servo recipe change. However, the simplicity of a mechanical indexer (no servo drives, no controllers, no software) appeals to plants with limited controls expertise.</p>
<p>Mechanical indexers excel in high-speed pharmaceutical blister packing, cartoning, and tube filling where the motion profile never changes. Companies like Destaco (Camco division) and Ferguson Machine manufacture precision mechanical indexers with decades of installed base in pharmaceutical and cosmetics packaging.</p>
<h3>Pallet Transfer (Workpiece Carrier) Indexing</h3>
<p>Pallet transfer systems carry product on machined pallets (workpiece carriers) that ride on a conveyor loop. The pallet provides a stable, repeatable reference surface, locating pins, nests, or fixtures on the pallet hold the product in a known position. The conveyor indexes the pallet to each workstation, and the pallet's precision fixturing delivers ±0.05mm repeatability at each station.</p>
<p>mk North America's VersaMove, Bosch Rexroth's TS 2plus, and Dorner's FlexMove pallet systems are widely used in pharmaceutical, medical device, and electronics assembly. These systems combine belt or chain conveyor transport with precision locating at each station using pop-up pins, clamps, or servo-positioned stops. The pallet circulates in a loop, loading at station one, indexing through process stations, unloading at the final station, and returning empty to the start.</p>
<p>Pallet transfer indexing suits operations where the same conveyor must handle multiple product variants. Different pallet fixtures accommodate different product shapes. Changeover involves swapping pallets rather than adjusting the conveyor. This approach is common in <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-lift/">packaging line integration</a> for kitting, assembly, and multi-step filling operations.</p>
<h3>Pneumatic Stop Indexing</h3>
<p>The simplest form of indexing uses a continuously running conveyor with pneumatic pop-up stops. The conveyor runs at constant speed. A pneumatic cylinder raises a stop blade into the product path, holding the product at the station. When the downstream operation completes, the stop retracts and the product advances to the next station.</p>
<p>Pneumatic stop indexing achieves ±1.0–2.0mm positioning accuracy, limited by product momentum, belt slip under the stopped product, and cylinder response time. This approach costs significantly less than servo indexing and works well for operations that tolerate moderate positional variation. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> with zone stops use this same principle to create multi-zone indexing behavior. Shuttleworth's slip-torque conveyors are designed for this mode, the belt slips under stopped product without building pressure or scuffing.</p>
