<h2 id="motion-heading">Motion Profiles Explained</h2>
<p>The motion profile defines how the indexing conveyor accelerates, cruises, and decelerates during each index cycle. Three profiles cover most packaging applications:</p>
<h3>Trapezoidal Profile</h3>
<p>The trapezoidal profile accelerates at a constant rate, cruises at maximum speed, and decelerates at a constant rate. The velocity-time graph forms a trapezoid. This profile produces the shortest possible index time for a given maximum speed and acceleration. The trade-off is jerk, abrupt transitions between acceleration, cruise, and deceleration phases create mechanical shock. Trapezoidal profiles suit rigid, heavy products (cases, cartons, pallets) that resist tipping.</p>
<h3>S-Curve Profile</h3>
<p>The S-curve profile ramps acceleration gradually, eliminating the abrupt jerk of trapezoidal motion. The velocity-time graph shows smooth transitions at the start and end of acceleration and deceleration phases. S-curve profiles take 10–15% longer than trapezoidal profiles for the same index distance but produce significantly less product disturbance. This profile is standard for bottles, containers, and any tall or top-heavy product that tends to topple under sudden acceleration.</p>
<h3>Modified Sine (Cycloidal) Profile</h3>
<p>The modified sine profile produces the smoothest possible motion, zero jerk at all points in the cycle. The acceleration curve follows a sine wave. This profile takes the longest time for a given index distance but provides the gentlest handling. Modified sine profiles suit fragile products (glass bottles, decorated cakes, filled cups) and precision operations where vibration at the dwell position must be minimized.</p>
<p>Servo controllers from Allen-Bradley (Rockwell), Siemens, Beckhoff, and B&amp;R offer all three profiles as standard selections. Custom cam profiles can combine elements, aggressive acceleration with gentle deceleration, for example, to optimize throughput for specific product characteristics.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Where Indexing Conveyors Work</h2>
<h3>Filling Stations</h3>
<p>Liquid and granular filling machines require containers to stop precisely under fill nozzles. Servo indexing conveyors position bottles, jars, or pouches under multi-head fillers with ±0.5mm accuracy. The dwell time matches the fill cycle, 0.5 to 3.0 seconds depending on product viscosity and fill volume. Overflow fillers, piston fillers, and net-weight fillers all rely on indexing conveyors for container positioning. The indexing conveyor feeds from a <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">laning or singulating conveyor</a> that delivers containers in single file at the correct pitch.</p>
<h3>Labeling Stations</h3>
<p>Pressure-sensitive labelers apply labels to products moving at a controlled speed or stopped at a fixed position. Wrap-around labeling requires the product to rotate at a consistent speed while the label applies, a continuous-motion indexer or timing screw provides this. Front-and-back labeling requires the product to stop momentarily while two label heads apply simultaneously. Servo belt indexers handle both modes with recipe changes. Dorner's Precision Move conveyors serve labeling applications across <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food</a>, beverage, and pharmaceutical lines.</p>
<h3>Pick-and-Place &amp; Robotic Loading</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">Tray loading</a> systems use indexing conveyors to position trays under pick-and-place heads. The tray indexes one pitch (one tray width), the pick-and-place loads product, then the tray indexes again. Servo indexing provides recipe-based tray pitch changes for different tray sizes. For robotic systems using vision tracking, the conveyor runs continuously rather than indexing, but the upstream product infeed often uses an indexing conveyor to meter product spacing.</p>
<h3>Assembly &amp; Kitting</h3>
<p>Pallet transfer indexing conveyors carry components through multi-station assembly cells. Each station adds a component, inspects, or tests. Medical device assembly, consumer electronics assembly, and automotive component kitting all use pallet indexers. mk North America's VersaMove system and Bosch Rexroth's TS 2plus are standard platforms for these applications. The pallet's precision fixturing ensures repeatable positioning at each station without re-referencing.</p>
<h3>Inspection &amp; Quality Control</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-functions/inspection-detection/">Vision inspection systems</a> require product to pause under cameras for image capture. Servo indexing conveyors dwell product at the camera station for 50–200ms, long enough for camera exposure and image processing. <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">Reject systems</a> downstream remove product that fails inspection. The indexing conveyor synchronizes with the vision system's trigger signal and the reject actuator's response time.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting an Indexing Conveyor</h2>
<p>Indexing conveyor selection requires matching five parameters to the downstream operation:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Required positioning accuracy.</strong> Labeling (±1mm) tolerates pneumatic stops. Robotic pick-and-place (±0.5mm) needs servo belt indexing. Precision assembly (±0.1mm) demands pallet transfer systems.</li>
<li><strong>Throughput requirement (CPM).</strong> Calculate cycle time = index time + dwell time. Ensure the indexing conveyor's maximum index speed supports the required cycle rate with 15% headroom.</li>
<li><strong>Product characteristics.</strong> Tall bottles need S-curve or modified sine profiles. Heavy cases tolerate trapezoidal. Fragile products demand soft acceleration and deceleration. Round containers may need timing screws instead of belt indexers.</li>
<li><strong>SKU count and changeover frequency.</strong> Single-format lines suit mechanical indexers (lowest cost, highest reliability). Multi-SKU lines need servo with recipe management. High-mix, low-volume lines need pallet transfer with swappable fixtures.</li>
<li><strong>Environment.</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary washdown</a> environments require stainless steel frames, IP69K drives, and food-grade belting. Cleanroom environments require low-particulate designs with enclosed drives. Standard industrial environments accept painted steel with standard IP54 enclosures.</li>
</ol>
<p>Discuss these parameters with manufacturers early in the project. Dorner, mk North America, and Shuttleworth all offer application engineering support to size indexing conveyors for specific packaging line requirements. For budgeting, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="integration-heading">PLC &amp; Controls Integration</h2>
<p>Indexing conveyors integrate with the line PLC through either a dedicated motion controller or a servo drive with built-in motion capability. The PLC sends a "start index" command, and the servo controller executes the stored motion profile. A "motion complete" signal returns to the PLC when the conveyor reaches the dwell position, triggering the downstream operation.</p>
<p>Modern servo drives from Allen-Bradley (Kinetix series), Siemens (Sinamics), and Beckhoff (AX series) support EtherNet/IP, PROFINET, and EtherCAT networks for real-time coordination between the indexer and other line equipment. This synchronization is critical when the indexing conveyor must coordinate with a filler, labeler, or robot that also has servo-driven axes.</p>
<p>For a broader view of controls architecture, sensor integration, and Industry 4.0 connectivity for conveyor systems, see the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems guide</a>.</p>
